{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773252820,"nonce":"X7RP2PUJ71sQP7Z5erCBSsEDIHQ=","title":"Reviewer verdict not extracted from conversation.jsonl","message":"The watcher `_check_reviewer_done()` calls `_extract_reviewer_verdict()` which reads `conversation.jsonl` looking for `@nightshift approve/revise` commands. But the session runner never writes the full assistant text block to conversation.jsonl — it only writes `@@LOG@@` content as `thought` entries and `@@CHECKPOINT@@` as checkpoints. The `@nightshift revise` or `@nightshift approve` command that appears in the assistant output between markers is never recorded.\n\n## Impact\n\nThe automated review pipeline is completely broken. Reviewer sessions finish and reach `waiting:review`, but the watcher cannot determine the verdict. Coder sessions stay stuck at `reviewing` indefinitely. Currently blocking issues 1fe3a37 and 3da4acc.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `review-1fe3a37c2269/conversation.jsonl` has no entry containing `@nightshift`\n- `review-1fe3a37c2269/raw-output.log` does contain `@nightshift approve` in the stream-json output\n- Same for `review-3da4accfdaef`\n\n## Fix\n\nThe session runner in `core/session.py` needs to write assistant text blocks (not just marker content) to `conversation.jsonl`. Specifically, the text content where `@nightshift` commands appear must be captured. Options:\n\n1. Write all assistant text messages to conversation.jsonl (not just marker-extracted content)\n2. Or: specifically detect `@nightshift` commands in the stream and write those entries\n\nOption 1 is simpler and more robust. The conversation log should reflect what the agent actually said.\n\n## Files\n- `core/session.py` — `SessionRunner` stream processing, write assistant text to conversation.jsonl\n- `core/state.py` — `StateManager` may need a method to write assistant messages\n- `tests/` — test that @nightshift commands appear in conversation.jsonl after session","files":null}]}